      <span class="tg-hero__type">Topical Study · Doctrine, Sacrament &amp; Order</span>
      <h1 class="tg-hero__title">How the Church Took Shape</h1>
      <p class="tg-hero__intro">
        Open a church door today and almost everything inside has a date attached. The
        creed recited, the number of sacraments, the man (or council, or congregation)
        in charge — each took its present form at a knowable moment, under knowable
        pressure. This study traces the three great structures of Christianity —
        <strong>doctrine</strong>, <strong>sacrament</strong>, and
        <strong>order</strong> — from their New Testament roots through the centuries
        that shaped them: when each developed, why, what Scripture says, where the
        great divisions fell, how the branches differ now, and the through line that
        runs unbroken through the faithful of every age.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tg-hero__key-verse">
        <p>Beloved, although I made every effort to write to you about the salvation we
           share, I felt it necessary to write and urge you to contend earnestly for
           the faith entrusted once for all to the saints.</p>
        <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 3">Jude 3</a></cite>
      </blockquote>
