           SECTION 1: HOW TO READ WHAT DEVELOPED
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="how-to-read">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">How to Read What Developed</h2>

        <p>Nothing in this study is settled by pretending the church fell from heaven
        fully formed. The New Testament church had no printed Bible, no Nicene Creed,
        no seven-sacrament list, no pope, no denominations. Everything that came later
        came <em>from somewhere</em>, and the honest questions are the same for every
        item on the shelf:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Question 1</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">When did it appear?</div>
          <p>Not when do we first <em>imagine</em> it, but when do the documents first
          show it. A practice attested in AD 100 and a doctrine defined in 1215 are
          different kinds of things, and dating them honestly is the first act of
          respect toward the truth. The dates in this study are the ones the primary
          sources themselves support.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Question 2</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Why did it develop?</div>
          <p>Almost nothing developed idly. Creeds were hammered out against specific
          heresies; the canon was listed because forgers and editors forced the issue;
          the office of bishop hardened under persecution and false teaching; sacramental
          definitions answered real disputes. Knowing the pressure that produced a
          structure is half of understanding it — and half of judging whether the
          structure outgrew its purpose.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Question 3</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">What does Scripture actually say?</div>
          <p>For every development there is a biblical text claimed in its support. Some
          claims are strong (the deity of Christ), some are real but contested (infant
          baptism), some are late readings projected backward (much of the papal
          argument). This study puts the supporting texts on the table each time, so you
          can weigh the claim yourself — like the Bereans, who "examined the Scriptures
          every day to see if these teachings were true"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:11">Acts 17:11</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Development or departure?</h3>

        <p>The New Testament itself carries both warnings at once. Tradition is
        <em>commanded</em>: "Therefore, brothers, stand firm and cling to the traditions
        we taught you, whether by speech or by letter"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Thessalonians 2:15">2 Thessalonians 2:15</a>;
        cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:2">1 Corinthians 11:2</a>). And
        tradition is <em>condemned</em>: "You have disregarded the commandment of God to
        keep the tradition of men"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 7:8">Mark 7:8</a>;
        cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 15:3-9">Matthew 15:3–9</a>). So the question
        is never "tradition, yes or no?" — no church on earth is without it — but
        <em>which</em> traditions carry the apostolic deposit forward and which bury it.
        Paul's charge to Timothy assumes the deposit can be both guarded and lost:
        "Guard the treasure entrusted to you"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 6:20">1 Timothy 6:20</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 1:13-14">2 Timothy 1:13–14</a>).</p>

        <p>The classic patristic test was framed by Vincent of Lérins in 434, writing
        after a century of doctrinal storms:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>Moreover, in the Catholic Church itself, all possible care must be taken,
          that we hold that faith which has been believed everywhere, always, by all…
          This rule we shall observe if we follow universality, antiquity, consent.</p>
          <cite>Vincent of Lérins, <em>Commonitory</em> II.6 (NPNF2 XI) · <a href="../../library/read/vincent-of-lerins-commonitory/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Vincent's canon is a useful sieve — it catches genuine novelties — but notice
        that it cannot be the final authority, because several doctrines every branch
        now confesses (the full Nicene Trinity, the two-natures definition) were
        precisely <em>not</em> yet confessed "everywhere, always, by all" while they
        were being fought for. The strongest case that doctrine legitimately grows —
        John Henry Newman's <em>Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine</em>
        (1845) — argues that a true development unfolds what was always present in seed,
        the way an oak unfolds an acorn. The Protestant reply is not that nothing
        develops, but that every development must remain answerable to the seed:
        Scripture stays the norm that judges the growth, "so that the man of God may be
        complete, fully equipped for every good work"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 3:16-17">2 Timothy 3:16–17</a>). This study
        works with that distinction throughout: <strong>development</strong> unfolds
        what Scripture teaches; <strong>departure</strong> adds to it, contradicts it,
        or buries it. Where a given structure falls is often the exact line along which
        Christendom later split.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The pattern to watch for</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Again and again the sequence runs: <em>practice first, crisis second, definition third.</em> Christians worshipped Jesus for three centuries before Nicaea defined how that squares with one God. They baptized and broke bread for a millennium before anyone defined a mechanism. Definitions are the church's scar tissue — nearly every one marks the place where a wound forced precision. That is why heresy, strange as it sounds, has been called the mother of theology: the false teachers set the exam questions; the church's answers became the creeds.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /how-to-read -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: RULE OF FAITH, CREEDS, CANON
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="rule-canon">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Doctrine I — The Rule of Faith, the Creeds, and the Canon</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Creeds before creeds: the confessions inside the New Testament</h3>

        <p>The instinct to compress the faith into a short, fixed confession is not a
        later invention — it is visible inside the New Testament itself. Paul quotes
        what most scholars recognize as an already-traditional formula, received within
        a few years of the crucifixion:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For what I received I passed on to you as of first importance: that Christ
          died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that He was buried, that He
          was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures…
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 15:3-4">1 Corinthians 15:3–4</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Other embedded confessions include the baptismal formula "Jesus is Lord"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 10:9">Romans 10:9</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:3">1 Corinthians 12:3</a>), the hymn of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:6-11">Philippians 2:6–11</a>, the "trustworthy
        sayings" of the Pastorals
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 1:15">1 Timothy 1:15</a>), and the compact
        "mystery of godliness" in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:16">1 Timothy 3:16</a>. Jude calls this
        deposit "the faith entrusted once for all to the saints"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 3">Jude 3</a>). So when later Christians wrote
        creeds, they were extending a biblical practice: the faith was always meant to
        be <em>summarizable</em>, confessed aloud, and handed on
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:2">2 Timothy 2:2</a>).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The rule of faith (2nd century)</h3>

        <p><strong>When and why:</strong> by the mid-100s the church faced teachers —
        the Gnostics, and Marcion with his edited scriptures — who used Christian
        vocabulary while gutting its content. Against them the churches appealed to a
        short summary of apostolic preaching, the <em>rule of faith</em>, taught to
        every convert at baptism. Irenaeus of Lyons, writing around 180, records it:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>The church, though dispersed throughout the whole world, even to the ends
          of the earth, has received from the apostles and their disciples this faith:
          [She believes] in one God, the Father Almighty, Maker of heaven, and earth,
          and the sea, and all things that are in them; and in one Christ Jesus, the
          Son of God, who became incarnate for our salvation; and in the Holy Spirit…</p>
          <cite>Irenaeus, <em>Against Heresies</em> I.10.1 (ANF I) · <a href="../../library/read/irenaeus-against-heresies/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Notice the shape: Father, Son, Spirit — the outline of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19">Matthew 28:19</a>, expanded. The rule of
        faith is the bridge between the New Testament's embedded confessions and the
        formal creeds. Out of the Roman church's baptismal questions grew the <em>Old
        Roman Symbol</em> (2nd century), which by roughly the 8th century reached the
        wording we call the <strong>Apostles' Creed</strong> — apostolic in content,
        not in authorship, and historically a Western creed (the East never adopted it
        liturgically). The <strong>Nicene Creed</strong> (325, expanded 381) came by a
        different road — conciliar definition against specific error — and is the one
        creed confessed by Catholic, Orthodox, and the historic Protestant churches
        alike. Both texts are in the site Library:
        <a href="../../library/read/apostles-creed/">Apostles' Creed</a> ·
        <a href="../../library/read/nicene-creed/">Nicene Creed</a>.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The canon of Scripture</h3>

        <p><strong>The Old Testament</strong> the church inherited: Jesus argued from
        "the Law of Moses, the Prophets, and the Psalms"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 24:44">Luke 24:44</a>) as a recognized body of
        Scripture, and the apostles preached from it
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:2-3">Acts 17:2–3</a>). <strong>The New
        Testament</strong> books functioned as Scripture from remarkably early — Peter
        already classes Paul's letters with "the rest of the Scriptures"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 3:15-16">2 Peter 3:15–16</a>), and Paul quotes
        a saying of Jesus recorded in Luke as "Scripture"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 5:18">1 Timothy 5:18</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 10:7">Luke 10:7</a>). The letters were written to
        be read aloud in the assemblies and circulated
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 4:16">Colossians 4:16</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Thessalonians 5:27">1 Thessalonians 5:27</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 1:3">Revelation 1:3</a>).</p>

        <p><strong>Why lists became necessary:</strong> nobody drew up a canon list in
        peacetime. Around 140 Marcion published the first known list — a mutilated Luke
        and ten edited Paulines, the whole Old Testament rejected — and the church had
        to answer. Gnostic groups produced rival "gospels" under apostolic names. Later,
        under Diocletian's persecution (303), imperial officers demanded the church's
        books for burning, and which books a Christian would die rather than surrender
        became a live question. The responses trace a clear arc:</p>

        <div class="tg-timeline">
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">c. 140</span><span><strong>Marcion's truncated canon</strong> forces the question — the church must say which books carry apostolic authority</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">c. 170–200</span><span>The <strong>Muratorian fragment</strong> — earliest surviving orthodox canon list (Rome): four Gospels, Acts, Paul, most of the rest, with a few edges still discussed</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">c. 325</span><span><strong>Eusebius</strong> sorts the books into acknowledged, disputed, and rejected — the edges (Hebrews, James, 2 Peter, 2–3 John, Jude, Revelation) are named, the core has long been fixed</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">367</span><span><strong>Athanasius's 39th Festal Letter</strong> — the first surviving list of exactly our 27 New Testament books, calling them the "springs of salvation"</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">393 / 397</span><span>Regional councils of <strong>Hippo and Carthage</strong> ratify the 27-book list already in use across the churches</span></div>
        </div>

        <p>The criteria the churches applied were consistent: <em>apostolicity</em>
        (written by an apostle or an apostle's companion), <em>orthodoxy</em>
        (agreement with the rule of faith), <em>catholicity</em> (received by the
        churches broadly, not one sect's private text), and <em>usage</em> (read in
        worship from the beginning). Two clarifications cut through most modern
        confusion:</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Recognized, not created</span>
          <p>No council "voted books into the Bible." (Nicaea, despite the novelists,
          never discussed the canon at all.) The 4th-century lists ratified what the
          churches had been reading in worship for generations; the core — four
          Gospels, Acts, Paul — was never seriously contested anywhere in the orthodox
          church. The church no more created the canon than a scale creates the weight
          it reports. As Jesus put it, "My sheep hear My voice"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:27">John 10:27</a>): the church's role was
          recognition of the voice already speaking in the books.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Where the branches differ: the Old Testament's edges</span>
          <p>The early church mostly read the Greek Septuagint, which carried additional
          books (Tobit, Judith, Wisdom, Sirach, 1–2 Maccabees, and others). Jerome
          (c. 400) distinguished them from the Hebrew canon as "ecclesiastical" books —
          read for edification, not for establishing doctrine — and that double view
          ran right through the Middle Ages. At the Reformation the lines hardened:
          <strong>Rome</strong> (Trent, 1546) defined the deuterocanon as fully
          canonical; <strong>Protestants</strong> kept the Hebrew canon, with Luther
          printing the Apocrypha between the Testaments as "useful and good to read";
          the <strong>Orthodox</strong> receive a still broader Septuagint canon,
          while treating the additional books as secondary in authority. The 27-book
          New Testament, by contrast, is identical in every branch.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /rule-canon -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: TRINITY AND CHRIST
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="trinity-christ">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Doctrine II — The Trinity and the Person of Christ</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Bible's raw material</h3>

        <p>The word "Trinity" is not in the Bible, and no verse states the doctrine in
        one sentence. What Scripture gives instead is a set of facts that can only all
        be held together one way. First, uncompromising monotheism: "Hear, O Israel:
        The LORD our God, the LORD is One"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 6:4">Deuteronomy 6:4</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 45:5">Isaiah 45:5</a>). Second, the full deity
        of the Son: "In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the
        Word was God" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 1:1">John 1:1</a>); Thomas's cry
        "My Lord and my God!" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 20:28">John 20:28</a>);
        "in Christ all the fullness of the Deity dwells in bodily form"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 2:9">Colossians 2:9</a>;
        cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 1:3">Hebrews 1:3</a>,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 1:8">8</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:6">Philippians 2:6</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 2:13">Titus 2:13</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="John 8:58">John 8:58</a>). Third, the personhood and
        deity of the Spirit: lying to the Spirit is lying to God
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 5:3-4">Acts 5:3–4</a>); he teaches, intercedes,
        can be grieved (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 14:26">John 14:26</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:26-27">Romans 8:26–27</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:30">Ephesians 4:30</a>). Fourth, the three
        named together as one divine reality: the baptismal formula — one "name," three
        persons (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19">Matthew 28:19</a>) — the
        apostolic blessing (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 13:14">2 Corinthians 13:14</a>),
        the one Spirit, one Lord, one God of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:4-6">1 Corinthians 12:4–6</a>, and the
        scene at Jesus' baptism, where Son, Spirit, and Father's voice appear at once
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 3:16-17">Matthew 3:16–17</a>).</p>

        <p>The doctrine of the Trinity is not an addition to these texts; it is the
        only statement that refuses to drop any of them. Every early heresy was a
        simplification — a way of keeping some of the verses by sacrificing others.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">When and why the definitions came</h3>

        <p>The <em>worship</em> came first. Around 112 the pagan governor Pliny reported
        to Trajan that Christians met before dawn and "sang a hymn to Christ as to a
        god"; the church prayed to Jesus from the beginning
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 7:59">Acts 7:59</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 16:22">1 Corinthians 16:22</a>). The
        <em>vocabulary</em> came later: Theophilus of Antioch uses the Greek
        <em>trias</em> of God around 180, and Tertullian coins the Latin
        <em>trinitas</em> — three persons, one substance — around 210. The
        <em>definitions</em> came last, and each was forced:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Against modalism (3rd century)</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">One actor wearing three masks?</div>
          <p>Sabellius and others taught that Father, Son, and Spirit are one person in
          three successive roles. The teaching dies at Jesus' baptism — the Son does not
          descend on himself while providing his own voice from heaven
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 3:16-17">Matthew 3:16–17</a>) — and in
          Gethsemane, where the Son's will is yielded to the Father's
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:42">Luke 22:42</a>;
          cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="John 17:5">John 17:5</a>). The church rejected it
          in the 3rd century, and every branch still does.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Against Arius (318–381)</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Is the Son a creature — even the highest one?</div>
          <p>Around 318 Arius, a presbyter of Alexandria, began teaching that the Son
          was God's first and greatest creature: "there was when he was not." The
          dispute engulfed the empire, and Constantine summoned the church's first
          ecumenical council at <strong>Nicaea (325)</strong>, which confessed the Son
          "begotten, not made, being of one substance (<em>homoousios</em>) with the
          Father" — deliberately un-evadable language
          (<a href="../../library/read/nicaea-i/">read the creed and canons in the Library</a>).
          The fight then ran another fifty years, with Athanasius exiled five times for
          refusing to soften the word, before the Council of
          <strong>Constantinople (381)</strong> settled the matter and expanded the
          third article — the Spirit as "the Lord and Giver of Life… who with the
          Father and the Son together is worshipped and glorified" — drawing on Basil's
          defense of the Spirit's deity
          (<a href="../../library/read/basil-on-holy-spirit/"><em>On the Holy Spirit</em>, 375</a>).
          The result is the Nicene Creed as every liturgical church still recites it
          (<a href="../../library/read/nicene-creed/">read in the Library</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Why a non-biblical word became necessary</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The Arians could sign nearly any purely biblical phrase while privately reinterpreting it — "Son of God," "firstborn," "Lord" all had Arian readings. <em>Homoousios</em> was chosen precisely because it could not be signed dishonestly. The principle matters for every later council: technical vocabulary in the creeds is not speculation added to Scripture, but a fence built where evasion had been found. The authority remains Scripture's; the new word only guards what texts like <a class="ref" data-ref="John 1:1">John 1:1</a> and <a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 2:9">Colossians 2:9</a> already say.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The person of Christ: to Chalcedon (381–451)</h3>

        <p>Settling that the Son is fully God raised the next question: how does full
        deity live in one person with full humanity — "the Word became flesh and made
        His dwelling among us" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 1:14">John 1:14</a>), born
        of a woman (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 4:4">Galatians 4:4</a>), growing,
        hungering, weeping, dying
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 2:52">Luke 2:52</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="John 11:35">John 11:35</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 2:14-17">Hebrews 2:14–17</a>)? Three
        simplifications were tried and rejected. <strong>Apollinaris</strong> proposed
        deity wearing a human body without a human mind — condemned in 381, on the
        principle Gregory of Nazianzus stated: what Christ has not assumed, he has not
        healed (Epistle 101); a half-humanity could only half-save
        (cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 2:17">Hebrews 2:17</a>).
        <strong>Nestorius</strong> was heard as splitting Christ into two sons — the
        divine Word and the man Jesus in partnership — against which the Council of
        <strong>Ephesus (431)</strong> insisted the child Mary bore is the one divine
        person of the Word (<em>Theotokos</em>, "God-bearer" — a statement about who
        Jesus is, not an elevation of Mary;
        <a href="../../library/read/ephesus-431/">read in the Library</a>).
        <strong>Eutyches</strong> then blended the natures into one new compound —
        deity diluted, humanity absorbed. The Council of <strong>Chalcedon (451)</strong>,
        guided by the Tome of Leo
        (<a href="../../library/read/leo-tome/">read in the Library</a>), answered with
        the definition that remains the shared christology of Catholic, Orthodox, and
        Protestant churches alike:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>…one and the same Son, the same perfect in Godhead, the same perfect in
          manhood, truly God and truly man… made known in two natures, which exist
          without confusion, without change, without division, without separation; the
          difference of the natures having been in no wise taken away by reason of the
          union, but rather the properties of each being preserved, and both concurring
          into one Person…</p>
          <cite>Definition of Chalcedon, 451 · <a href="../../library/read/chalcedon-451/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Notice the method: four negatives. Chalcedon does not explain the
        incarnation; it fences the mystery against the four known ways of getting it
        wrong. But precision had a price. The definition was rejected by churches of
        the East and of Egypt and Syria, producing the first lasting institutional
        splits in Christian history — the Church of the East after 431, and the
        Oriental Orthodox family after 451 (<a href="#splits">§ 10</a>).</p>

      </section><!-- /trinity-christ -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: SIN, GRACE, JUSTIFICATION
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="sin-grace">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Doctrine III — Sin, Grace, and Justification</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Bible's raw material</h3>

        <p>Scripture's diagnosis of the human condition is bleak and consistent: "every
        inclination of the thoughts of his heart was altogether evil all the time"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 6:5">Genesis 6:5</a>); "Surely I was brought
        forth in iniquity; I was sinful when my mother conceived me"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 51:5">Psalm 51:5</a>); "The heart is deceitful
        above all things" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 17:9">Jeremiah 17:9</a>).
        Paul systematizes: sin entered through one man and death spread to all
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 5:12-19">Romans 5:12–19</a>); we were "dead in
        your trespasses and sins" — and salvation is "by grace… through faith, and this
        not from yourselves; it is the gift of God, not by works"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:1-9">Ephesians 2:1–9</a>). Jesus is just
        as absolute: "No one can come to Me unless the Father who sent Me draws him"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 6:44">John 6:44</a>). And on the question of what
        makes a person right with God:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For we maintain that a man is justified by faith apart from works of the law.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 3:28">Romans 3:28</a>; cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:16">Galatians 2:16</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 4:5">Romans 4:5</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Alongside which stands James — "a man is justified by his deeds and not by
        faith alone" (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:24">James 2:24</a>) — using
        "justified" of the vindication that living faith produces
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:18">James 2:18</a>,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:26">26</a>). How to hold Paul and James in one
        doctrine has been the church's homework ever since; the full working-through is
        in the <a href="../justification/">Justification by Faith</a> study.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">When and why the definitions came</h3>

        <p>For three centuries the church preached grace and commanded obedience without
        systematizing how they relate — the fathers' energies were consumed by the
        Trinity. The crisis came around 411, when <strong>Pelagius</strong>, a British
        monk scandalized by lax Christians, taught that Adam's sin injured only Adam,
        that humans are born able to obey God completely, and that grace is mainly
        instruction and example. <strong>Augustine</strong> answered from Paul and from
        his own converted heart: the will itself is in bondage until grace frees it —
        "What do you have that you did not receive?"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 4:7">1 Corinthians 4:7</a>;
        cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="John 15:5">John 15:5</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:13">Philippians 2:13</a>). Councils at
        Carthage (418) and Ephesus (431) condemned Pelagianism, and after a century of
        softer "semi-Pelagian" proposals, the Second Council of
        <strong>Orange (529)</strong> fixed the Western church's settled answer:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>The sin of the first man has so impaired and weakened free will that no
          one thereafter can either love God as he ought or believe in God or do good
          for God's sake, unless the grace of divine mercy has preceded him… in every
          good work it is not we who take the initiative and are then assisted through
          the mercy of God, but God himself first inspires in us both faith in him and
          love for him without any previous good works of our own that deserve reward.</p>
          <cite>Council of Orange II, Concluding Definition, 529 · <a href="../../library/read/orange-529/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Two footnotes matter. Orange also rejected any notion that God predestines
        anyone <em>to</em> evil — grace-first, without fatalism. And, remarkably, the
        acts of Orange were lost for most of the Middle Ages (rediscovered only in
        1538), which helps explain how the medieval West could drift toward
        merit-language that its own council had ruled out. The Christian East, which
        never fought the Pelagian war, kept an older, less systematized frame:
        humanity inherits mortality and corruption from Adam (ancestral sin) rather
        than his guilt, and salvation is a lifelong synergy of grace and freed will
        aimed at real transformation
        (cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 1:4">2 Peter 1:4</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:12-13">Philippians 2:12–13</a>).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">How justification became the dividing line (1215–1547)</h3>

        <p>The medieval West embedded the forgiveness of sins inside the sacrament of
        penance: contrition, confession to a priest (made annually obligatory by the
        Fourth Lateran Council, 1215), absolution, and works of satisfaction. Around
        that system grew the theology of merit, purgatory as the place where
        satisfaction is completed, and — from the First Crusade (1095) onward —
        <strong>indulgences</strong>: remissions of penalty drawn, in the mature theory
        (Clement VI, 1343), from a treasury of merit administered by the pope. By 1517
        indulgences were being sold to fund St. Peter's basilica, with slogans
        promising the release of souls from purgatory — the spark for Luther's
        Ninety-Five Theses
        (<a href="../../library/read/luther-95-theses/">read in the Library</a>), whose
        first thesis quietly relocated repentance from the confessional to the whole
        of life: "when our Lord and Master Jesus Christ said <em>Repent</em>, he willed
        the entire life of believers to be one of repentance"
        (cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 4:17">Matthew 4:17</a>).</p>

        <p>What began as a protest about indulgences became, by 1520, a rediscovered
        doctrine: justification as God's verdict, received by faith alone, on account
        of Christ alone — righteousness counted to the believer as it was to Abraham
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 4:3-5">Romans 4:3–5</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 15:6">Genesis 15:6</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 5:21">2 Corinthians 5:21</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 3:9">Philippians 3:9</a>). The Augsburg
        Confession (1530) states it in a sentence:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>Our churches teach that men cannot be justified before God by their own
          strength, merits, or works, but are freely justified for Christ's sake,
          through faith… This faith God imputes for righteousness in His sight.</p>
          <cite>Augsburg Confession, Article IV, 1530 · <a href="../../library/read/augsburg-confession/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Rome's counter-definition came at the Council of <strong>Trent</strong>
        (Session VI, 1547): justification is not a bare verdict but an infusion of
        righteousness that makes a person actually just — begun by grace, increased by
        cooperation and the sacraments, forfeited by mortal sin, recoverable through
        penance — and the Reformation's "faith alone" was anathematized as Trent
        understood it. There the line has stood for nearly five centuries, though the
        1999 <em>Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification</em>
        (Lutheran–Catholic, later joined by Methodist, Anglican, and Reformed bodies)
        found real common ground in grace-alone language while honestly listing what
        still differs: whether the righteousness that justifies is Christ's, imputed,
        or the believer's own, infused — and with it the possibility of assurance
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:1">Romans 8:1</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:13">1 John 5:13</a>; see the
        <a href="../salvation-assurance/">Assurance of Salvation</a> study).</p>

      </section><!-- /sin-grace -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: WHAT IS A SACRAMENT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="sacrament-what">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Sacraments I — What They Are, and How Many</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The words and the idea</h3>

        <p>The New Testament's word is <em>mystērion</em> — "mystery," God's once-hidden
        purpose now revealed in Christ
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:9">Ephesians 1:9</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 3:3-6">3:3–6</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 1:26-27">Colossians 1:26–27</a>). The Latin
        Bible rendered it <em>sacramentum</em> — in Roman usage, a soldier's oath of
        allegiance — and the word stuck to the church's sacred rites. Augustine gave the
        West its working definition: a sacrament is a visible sign of invisible grace —
        "the word is added to the element, and it becomes a sacrament" (<em>Tractates
        on John</em> 80.3). The East simply kept calling them the <em>mysteries</em>.</p>

        <p>Two rites stand apart in Scripture because Christ himself instituted them
        with a command and a promise: <strong>baptism</strong> — "go and make disciples
        of all nations, baptizing them"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19">Matthew 28:19</a>) — and the
        <strong>supper</strong> — "do this in remembrance of Me"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:19">Luke 22:19</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:24-25">1 Corinthians 11:24–25</a>).
        Every branch of Christianity observes both. The question the centuries argued
        is what else belongs in the category, and what the rites actually do.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">How the count became seven (1150–1547)</h3>

        <p>For a thousand years no fixed number existed. The fathers used "mystery"
        generously — of the creed, the Lord's Prayer, footwashing, the sign of the
        cross; Augustine could call many things sacraments. As late as the 1120s Hugh
        of St. Victor catalogued dozens. The fixed <strong>seven</strong> — baptism,
        confirmation, eucharist, penance, extreme unction, orders, matrimony — comes
        from Peter Lombard's <em>Sentences</em> (c. 1150), the textbook on which every
        medieval theologian was trained. Once the schools adopted Lombard's list, the
        councils ratified it: Lyons II (1274), Florence (1439), and finally Trent
        (1547), which made the number a dogma. The Orthodox East, which had never
        legislated a count, gradually echoed "seven" in its 17th-century confessions
        under Western influence, while continuing to treat the boundary as soft — the
        whole life of the church is mystery.</p>

        <p>The Reformation applied a simple test — instituted by Christ for all
        Christians, with a visible sign and a gospel promise — and the list contracted
        to two:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>There are two Sacraments ordained of Christ our Lord in the Gospel, that
          is to say, Baptism, and the Supper of the Lord. Those five commonly called
          Sacraments… are not to be counted for Sacraments of the Gospel.</p>
          <cite>Thirty-Nine Articles, Article XXV, 1571 · <a href="../../library/read/39-articles/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Note what the Reformers did <em>not</em> say: that the other five name
        nothing real. Confession of sin, prayer for the sick, ordination, marriage —
        all remain biblical practices in every Protestant church; they were reclassified,
        not abolished. Here is each of the five, with its biblical anchor and its
        development:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Confirmation</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">From the apostles' hands to a separate rite.</div>
          <p>Anchor: the apostles laying hands on the baptized for the Spirit
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 8:14-17">Acts 8:14–17</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 19:5-6">Acts 19:5–6</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:2">Hebrews 6:2</a>). In the early church
          anointing and hand-laying belonged to the baptism service itself. The East
          kept it that way — infants are baptized, chrismated, and communed in one
          liturgy. In the West the bishop's part became detached (bishops could not
          attend every village baptism) and drifted years after baptism, becoming a
          separate sacrament of maturity; Protestant churches that kept confirmation
          treat it as public profession of faith, not a second channel of grace.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Penance / confession</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">From public restoration to the confessional.</div>
          <p>Anchor: Christ's grant of binding and loosing
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 20:23">John 20:23</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:15-18">Matthew 18:15–18</a>) and "confess
          your sins to one another"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 5:16">James 5:16</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 1:9">1 John 1:9</a>). The early form was
          <em>public</em>: grave post-baptismal sin meant open confession and a long,
          once-in-a-lifetime restoration (see the Church's 3rd-century fights over the
          lapsed — <a href="../../library/read/cyprian-on-the-lapsed/">Cyprian, <em>On
          the Lapsed</em></a>). Private, repeatable confession spread from the Irish
          monasteries (6th–7th centuries), and Lateran IV (1215) made annual private
          confession obligatory. Luther prized voluntary confession and absolution;
          most Protestants located confession in prayer to God and mutual
          accountability.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Anointing of the sick</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">From healing prayer to last rites — and back.</div>
          <p>Anchor: "Is any one of you sick? He should call the elders of the church
          to pray over him and anoint him with oil in the name of the Lord"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 5:14-15">James 5:14–15</a>;
          cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 6:13">Mark 6:13</a>). What James describes
          as healing prayer narrowed in the medieval West into "extreme unction," a
          deathbed rite; Vatican II (1962–65) deliberately renamed and restored it as
          anointing of the sick. Protestants kept the practice of James 5 without the
          sacramental category.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Orders</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Ordination — office as sacrament.</div>
          <p>Anchor: appointment to ministry by laying on of hands
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 4:14">1 Timothy 4:14</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 1:6">2 Timothy 1:6</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 6:6">Acts 6:6</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 13:3">13:3</a>). Whether ordination confers an
          indelible sacramental character (Rome), a true setting-apart within apostolic
          succession (Rome and the East), or a solemn recognition of calling and
          gifting (most Protestants) is really the hierarchy question — taken up in
          <a href="#order-nt">§ 8–9</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Matrimony</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">A creation ordinance read through one Latin word.</div>
          <p>Anchor: marriage is God's from Eden
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 2:24">Genesis 2:24</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 19:4-6">Matthew 19:4–6</a>), and Paul calls
          the union of husband and wife a great <em>mystērion</em> pointing to Christ
          and the church (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 5:31-32">Ephesians
          5:31–32</a>). The Vulgate's rendering — <em>sacramentum hoc magnum est</em> —
          eased marriage onto the sacramental list, formalized by the medieval councils
          and defended at Trent. Protestants honor marriage as a holy creation
          ordinance for all humanity rather than a church sacrament — which is also why
          the Reformers reopened marriage to clergy
          (cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:2">1 Timothy 3:2</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 9:5">1 Corinthians 9:5</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:4">Hebrews 13:4</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The deep question underneath the counting</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The real dispute was never arithmetic. It is whether grace is <em>conveyed through</em> the rites themselves when validly performed (Rome's <em>ex opere operato</em> — "by the work worked"), <em>offered in</em> them and received by faith (the classic Protestant instrument view — "sure witnesses and effectual signs of grace," as Article XXV puts it; "holy signs and seals of the covenant of grace," Westminster XXVII), or <em>remembered and professed</em> in them (the Baptist and free-church "ordinance" view). Where a church lands here quietly shapes everything else — its worship, its ministry, its assurance, even its architecture.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /sacrament-what -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: BAPTISM
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="baptism">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Sacraments II — Baptism</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The command and the claims Scripture makes for it</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Therefore go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of
          the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit, and teaching them to obey
          all that I have commanded you.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19-20">Matthew 28:19–20</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>From Pentecost onward baptism is the door of the church — commanded with
        repentance "for the forgiveness of your sins"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:38">Acts 2:38</a>), administered the same day
        to converts (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:41">Acts 2:41</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 8:36-38">8:36–38</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 16:33">16:33</a>). The New Testament speaks of it
        in startlingly strong terms: burial and resurrection with Christ
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 6:3-4">Romans 6:3–4</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 2:12">Colossians 2:12</a>), washing
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 22:16">Acts 22:16</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 3:5">Titus 3:5</a>), clothing with Christ
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 3:27">Galatians 3:27</a>), and — with
        Peter's own guard-rail attached — salvation: "baptism… now saves you also, not
        the removal of dirt from the body, but the pledge of a clear conscience toward
        God" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 3:21">1 Peter 3:21</a>). Whole
        households were baptized on a head's conversion
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 16:15">Acts 16:15</a>,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 16:33">33</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 1:16">1 Corinthians 1:16</a>) — the
        texts both sides of the later infant-baptism debate reach for first.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Earliest practice outside the New Testament</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>But concerning baptism, thus shall ye baptize. Having first recited all
          these things, baptize in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the
          Holy Spirit in living (running) water. But if thou hast not living water,
          then baptize in other water; and if thou art not able in cold, then in warm.
          But if thou hast neither, then pour water on the head thrice…</p>
          <cite>Didache 7 · c. AD 50–120 · <a href="../../library/read/didache/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The church's earliest manual already shows the practical spirit of the age:
        immersion in running water preferred, pouring permitted where water is scarce —
        the mode serves the sign. Candidates fasted, and the baptized alone came to the
        table (Didache 9).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">When infant baptism appears — and why</h3>

        <p>The New Testament records no infant baptism and no prohibition of it; both
        sides argue from inference. The documentary trail outside Scripture begins
        around 200, and its first witness is, tellingly, an objector: Tertullian
        (<em>On Baptism</em> 18) urges <em>delay</em> — "let them become Christians
        when they have become able to know Christ" — which proves the practice existed
        to object to. In the same generation Hippolytus's church order describes
        baptizing "little children" whose parents answer for them; Origen (c. 240)
        calls infant baptism a custom received from the apostles; and in 253 Cyprian
        and a council of 66 bishops ruled that baptism need not wait even until the
        eighth day of life. Through the 4th century many raised in Christian homes
        nonetheless deferred baptism for years (Constantine and Augustine among them),
        fearing post-baptismal sin. It was Augustine's doctrine of original sin —
        forged in the Pelagian controversy
        (<a href="#sin-grace">§ 4</a>) — that made infant baptism theologically
        systematic in the West, and from roughly the 5th century to the 16th, the
        baptism of infants was the near-universal practice of Christendom.</p>

        <p>The scriptural cases, at their strongest, run like this. <strong>For infant
        baptism</strong>: baptism replaces circumcision as the covenant sign
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 2:11-12">Colossians 2:11–12</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 17:7">Genesis 17:7</a>) — and the covenant
        sign was always given to infants of believers; "the promise belongs to you and
        your children" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:39">Acts 2:39</a>); the
        household baptisms; Jesus' welcome of infants as heirs of the kingdom
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 18:15-16">Luke 18:15–16</a>);
        believers' children called "holy"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 7:14">1 Corinthians 7:14</a>).
        <strong>For believers' baptism</strong>: every explicit baptism in Acts follows
        personal repentance and faith
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:38">Acts 2:38</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 8:12">8:12</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 18:8">18:8</a>); the sign's own meaning — burial
        and resurrection through faith
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 2:12">Colossians 2:12</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 6:3-4">Romans 6:3–4</a>) — presupposes a
        believer; the new covenant is defined by everyone in it knowing the Lord
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 31:31-34">Jeremiah 31:31–34</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 8:10-11">Hebrews 8:10–11</a>).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Reformation divide (1525) and the branches now</h3>

        <p>The Reformers kept infant baptism, re-grounded on covenant rather than on a
        bare mechanism — Westminster calls baptism "a sign and seal of the covenant of
        grace" administered also to "the infants of one, or both, believing parents"
        (<a href="../../library/read/westminster-confession/">Westminster Confession
        XXVIII</a>). The Anabaptists ("re-baptizers" — their enemies' name) broke the
        millennium-long consensus in Zurich in 1525, and said what no established
        church had said since the patristic era:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>Baptism shall be given to all those who have been taught repentance and
          the amendment of life and who believe truly that their sins are taken away
          through Christ… to all those who with such understanding desire and request
          it from us. Thereby is excluded all infant baptism.</p>
          <cite>Schleitheim Confession, Article I, 1527 · <a href="../../library/read/schleitheim-confession/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The stakes were ecclesiology itself: a church of confessors only, joined by
        adult decision, distinct from the state — and the price was blood. Felix Manz,
        an author of the Zurich movement, was drowned in the Limmat in 1527 by a
        Protestant government, mocked as a "third baptism." The English Baptists
        (from 1609, with immersion normative by the 1640s) carried believers' baptism
        into the mainstream of Protestantism
        (<a href="../../library/read/london-baptist-confession/">Second London
        Confession, 1689</a>), and through the Baptist, Restorationist, Pentecostal,
        and independent evangelical families it is now the practice of a large share
        of the Protestant world. Today: <strong>Catholic, Orthodox, Lutheran,
        Reformed/Presbyterian, Anglican, and Methodist</strong> churches baptize
        infants of believers (differing over exactly what the baptism effects —
        regeneration itself, or the covenant sign and seal); <strong>Baptist,
        Anabaptist-descended, Pentecostal, and most independent</strong> churches
        baptize on profession of faith, normally by immersion. Nearly all baptize
        with water in the triune name of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:19">Matthew 28:19</a> — and one ancient
        mutual recognition still quietly holds across most of the divide: "one Lord,
        one faith, one baptism"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:5">Ephesians 4:5</a>).</p>

      </section><!-- /baptism -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: THE LORD'S SUPPER
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="supper">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Sacraments III — The Lord's Supper</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The institution</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For I received from the Lord what I also passed on to you: The Lord Jesus, on
          the night He was betrayed, took bread, and when He had given thanks, He broke
          it and said, "This is My body, which is for you; do this in remembrance of
          Me." In the same way, after supper He took the cup, saying, "This cup is the
          new covenant in My blood; do this, as often as you drink it, in remembrance
          of Me." For as often as you eat this bread and drink this cup, you proclaim
          the Lord's death until He comes.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:23-26">1 Corinthians 11:23–26</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Written around AD 55 — before any Gospel — this is the earliest surviving
        account of the supper, and Paul says he "received" it as tradition already
        fixed. The Gospels record the institution in the Passover setting
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 26:26-29">Matthew 26:26–29</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 14:22-25">Mark 14:22–25</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:14-20">Luke 22:14–20</a>), and Paul adds the
        warnings that make the table serious: participation in the body and blood
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:16-17">1 Corinthians 10:16–17</a>),
        judgment for eating unworthily
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:27-29">1 Corinthians 11:27–29</a>).
        Whether John 6 — "unless you eat the flesh and drink the blood of the Son of
        Man, you have no life in you"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 6:53-54">John 6:53–54</a>) — speaks directly of
        the supper or of faith in Christ's atoning death (which the supper enacts) is
        itself part of the later debate; Jesus' own closing words there, "the words
        that I have spoken to you are spirit and they are life"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 6:63">John 6:63</a>), are quoted on both sides.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The early church at the table</h3>

        <p>The supper began inside an actual meal
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:20-22">1 Corinthians 11:20–22</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:46">Acts 2:46</a>) and by the early 2nd century
        had become a distinct morning rite. The Didache preserves the oldest eucharistic
        prayers we have:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>As this broken bread was scattered upon the mountains and being gathered
          together became one, so may Thy Church be gathered together from the ends of
          the earth into Thy kingdom… But let no one eat or drink of this eucharistic
          thanksgiving, but they that have been baptized into the name of the Lord.</p>
          <cite>Didache 9 · <a href="../../library/read/didache/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The language of the next generations is strikingly realist — because the
        battle of the hour was against docetists who denied Christ came in real flesh
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:2-3">1 John 4:2–3</a>). Ignatius, on the road
        to martyrdom around 108:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>They abstain from the Eucharist and from prayer, because they confess not
          the Eucharist to be the flesh of our Saviour Jesus Christ, which suffered for
          our sins, and which the Father, of His goodness, raised up again.</p>
          <cite>Ignatius, <em>To the Smyrnaeans</em> 7 (ANF I) · <a href="../../library/read/ignatius-smyrnaeans/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>— and to the Ephesians he calls the one bread "the medicine of immortality,
        and the antidote to prevent us from dying"
        (<a href="../../library/read/ignatius-ephesians/"><em>To the Ephesians</em> 20</a>).
        Justin Martyr (c. 155) told the emperor plainly that "not as common bread and
        common drink do we receive these," but as the flesh and blood of the incarnate
        Jesus (<em>First Apology</em> 66, ANF I). Yet the same centuries also speak
        freely of sign and figure — Tertullian calls the bread "a figure of my body,"
        and Augustine developed a whole theology of sacramental signs. Realist and
        symbolic registers coexisted for a thousand years without a defined mechanism,
        because no crisis had yet demanded one.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Definition in the West (831–1274) — and fracture (1517–1563)</h3>

        <div class="tg-timeline">
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">831–850s</span><span>First real Western debate: Paschasius Radbertus (the elements become the very body) vs. Ratramnus (truly received, but in figure and power) — monks of the same abbey</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1059/1079</span><span>Berengar of Tours forced to swear to an ultra-realist formula — the mechanism question now has official momentum</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1215</span><span><strong>Fourth Lateran Council</strong> uses the new term: the bread and wine are <em>transubstantiated</em> into Christ's body and blood</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1274</span><span>Aquinas gives the doctrine its precision: the whole <em>substance</em> changes while the <em>accidents</em> (appearance, taste) remain (<a href="../../library/read/aquinas-summa-part-iii/">Summa III</a>)</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1215–1500s</span><span>Practice follows doctrine: elevation of the host, eucharistic adoration, Corpus Christi festival (1264), the cup increasingly withheld from the laity, the mass understood as a propitiatory sacrifice offered by the priest</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1529</span><span><strong>Marburg</strong>: Luther and Zwingli agree on fourteen articles and split on the fifteenth — the supper. Luther chalks <em>Hoc est corpus meum</em> on the table and will not yield; Protestantism's first internal wall goes up</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1547–1563</span><span><strong>Trent</strong> reaffirms transubstantiation and the sacrifice of the mass against all Protestant views</span></div>
        </div>

        <p>All Reformers rejected transubstantiation and the mass-sacrifice — Christ
        "offered for all time one sacrifice for sins"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:12-14">Hebrews 10:12–14</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 9:25-28">9:25–28</a>) — and restored the cup
        to the people ("drink from it, <em>all of you</em>,"
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 26:27">Matthew 26:27</a>). But they could not
        agree among themselves on what the supper <em>is</em>, and that disagreement
        still maps the Protestant world:</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>View</th><th>What happens at the table</th><th>Held today by</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Transubstantiation</strong></td><td>The bread and wine become in substance the body and blood of Christ; the mass re-presents his one sacrifice</td><td>Roman Catholic</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Real presence as mystery</strong></td><td>The gifts truly become Christ's body and blood by the Spirit; the "how" is left undefined</td><td>Eastern &amp; Oriental Orthodox</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Sacramental union</strong></td><td>Christ's true body and blood are received "in, with, and under" the bread and wine, which remain bread and wine</td><td>Lutheran</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Spiritual presence</strong></td><td>Christ is truly fed upon by faith, the Spirit lifting believers to communion with the ascended Lord — "after an heavenly and spiritual manner" (Article XXVIII)</td><td>Reformed, Presbyterian, many Anglicans</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Memorial</strong></td><td>A commanded remembrance and proclamation of the cross, and a fellowship of the body — "do this in remembrance of Me"</td><td>Baptist, Anabaptist, Restorationist, most Pentecostal &amp; independent churches</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">What no branch disputes</span>
          <p>Every tradition keeps the table because Christ commanded it; every
          tradition proclaims his death in it "until He comes"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:26">1 Corinthians 11:26</a>); and
          every serious tradition fences it with self-examination
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:28">1 Corinthians 11:28</a>). The
          quarrel — and it is a real quarrel, not a misunderstanding — is over the mode
          of Christ's presence, not the fact of his command or the meaning of his
          cross.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /supper -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: NT PATTERN OF ORDER
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="order-nt">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Hierarchy I — The New Testament Pattern</h2>

        <p>Jesus left no organizational chart. He left apostles — eyewitnesses
        commissioned by him
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:21-22">Acts 1:21–22</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 9:1">1 Corinthians 9:1</a>) — and the
        apostles, as the churches multiplied, appointed local leadership:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          The reason I left you in Crete was that you would set in order what was
          unfinished and appoint elders in every town, as I directed you.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5">Titus 1:5</a>; cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 14:23">Acts 14:23</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Two ordinary offices appear, consistently plural and local.
        <strong>Elders</strong> (<em>presbyteroi</em>) — also called
        <strong>overseers</strong> (<em>episkopoi</em>, the word that became "bishop") —
        shepherd and teach the flock. That the two titles name one office in the New
        Testament is shown, not inferred: Paul summons the <em>elders</em> of Ephesus
        and tells them the Spirit made them <em>overseers</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:17">Acts 20:17</a>,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:28">28</a>); Titus is to appoint <em>elders</em>,
        "since an <em>overseer</em>… must be blameless"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5-7">Titus 1:5–7</a>); Peter exhorts the
        <em>elders</em> to shepherd, serving as <em>overseers</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:1-2">1 Peter 5:1–2</a>).
        <strong>Deacons</strong> serve the church's practical life
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 6:1-6">Acts 6:1–6</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:8-13">1 Timothy 3:8–13</a>), and a letter
        can address a whole church "together with the overseers and deacons"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:1">Philippians 1:1</a>) — plural
        overseers, one city. The qualifications for both offices are almost shockingly
        ordinary: character, sound doctrine, a well-ordered household
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:1-13">1 Timothy 3:1–13</a>) — no
        aristocracy, no priestly caste, no celibacy requirement ("the husband of but
        one wife," <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:2">1 Timothy 3:2</a>).</p>

        <p>Around the offices, the whole body ministers: Christ gave "apostles,
        prophets, evangelists, and shepherds and teachers <em>to equip the saints for
        works of ministry</em>"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:11-12">Ephesians 4:11–12</a>); every
        member is a gifted part of the body
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:4-11">1 Corinthians 12:4–11</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:4-8">Romans 12:4–8</a>); and the church as
        such — not a clerical class — is "a royal priesthood"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:9">1 Peter 2:9</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 1:6">Revelation 1:6</a>). And over every
        exercise of authority stands the Lord's own standard, the text by which all
        later hierarchy must be judged:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their
          superiors exercise authority over them. It shall not be this way among you.
          Instead, whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 20:25-26">Matthew 20:25–26</a>; cf. <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 23:8-12">Matthew 23:8–12</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:3">1 Peter 5:3</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 1:24">2 Corinthians 1:24</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>At the close of the 1st century the picture is unchanged. The Didache:
        "Appoint for yourselves therefore bishops and deacons worthy of the Lord"
        (<a href="../../library/read/didache/">Didache 15</a>) — two offices, chosen by
        the congregation. And Clement of Rome, writing to Corinth around 96, still uses
        "bishop" and "presbyter" interchangeably while insisting ministers appointed
        "with the consent of the whole church" not be unjustly deposed
        (<a href="../../library/read/clement-first-epistle/">1 Clement 44</a>).</p>

      </section><!-- /order-nt -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: FROM ELDERS TO PATRIARCHS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="order-growth">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Hierarchy II — From Elders to Patriarchs</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The three-fold ministry appears (c. 108–110)</h3>

        <p>The first witness to a different shape is Ignatius of Antioch, writing seven
        letters on his way to execution around 108–110. In his churches of Syria and
        Asia Minor there is now <em>one</em> bishop in each city, distinct from and
        over the presbyters, with the deacons third:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          <p>See that ye all follow the bishop, even as Jesus Christ does the Father,
          and the presbytery as ye would the apostles… Let no man do anything connected
          with the church without the bishop. Let that be deemed a proper Eucharist,
          which is [administered] either by the bishop, or by one to whom he has
          entrusted it. Wherever the bishop shall appear, there let the multitude [of
          the people] also be; even as, wherever Jesus Christ is, there is the catholic
          church.</p>
          <cite>Ignatius, <em>To the Smyrnaeans</em> 8 (ANF I) · <a href="../../library/read/ignatius-smyrnaeans/">read in the Library</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p><strong>Why?</strong> Ignatius tells us himself: the churches were being
        torn by docetic and Judaizing teachers, and one visible shepherd per city was
        the rallying point — one bishop, one eucharist, one altar, no splinter
        assemblies. Notice also what Ignatius does <em>not</em> say: he never grounds
        the bishop's office in succession from Peter or in a command of Christ; his
        argument is typological (the bishop images God the Father) and practical
        (unity). The monarchical episcopate spread through the 2nd century — by
        Irenaeus's day (c. 180) it was universal, and had acquired a new argument:
        <em>apostolic succession</em>, the public line of teachers back to the
        apostles, wielded against Gnostics who claimed secret traditions
        (<a href="../../library/read/irenaeus-against-heresies/"><em>Against
        Heresies</em> III.3</a>). Centuries later Jerome — no radical — could still
        state the original arrangement flatly: the churches were at first "governed by
        a common council of the presbyters," and one was set over the rest "by custom
        rather than by the Lord's actual arrangement," as a remedy for schism
        (<em>Commentary on Titus</em> 1:5; Letter 146).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Priest, altar, and the widening clergy–laity divide (200–500)</h3>

        <p>With Cyprian of Carthage (c. 250) the vocabulary shifts: the bishop is a
        <em>priest</em>, the table an <em>altar</em>, the supper a <em>sacrifice</em> —
        Old Testament categories the New Testament had reserved for Christ himself
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 7:23-27">Hebrews 7:23–27</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:11-14">10:11–14</a>) and for the whole
        priestly people (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:5">1 Peter 2:5</a>,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:9">9</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:1">Romans 12:1</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:15-16">Hebrews 13:15–16</a>). For Cyprian
        the bishop <em>is</em> the church's principle of unity: "the episcopate is one…
        if a man does not hold this unity of the Church, does he think that he holds
        the faith?"
        (<a href="../../library/read/cyprian-on-unity/"><em>On the Unity of the
        Church</em></a>). After Constantine (313), the structure scaled with the
        empire: bishops gained civil standing, dress, and courts; the city bishop
        became a diocesan ruler. The Council of Nicaea's canons already assume
        <strong>metropolitans</strong> (provincial head bishops) and confirm the
        super-regional authority of Alexandria, Rome, and Antioch
        (<a href="../../library/read/nicaea-i/">canons 4 and 6</a>);
        Constantinople was ranked just after Rome as the "new Rome" in 381
        (<a href="../../library/read/constantinople-i/">canon 3</a>), a status
        Chalcedon expanded in 451 over Rome's protest
        (<a href="../../library/read/chalcedon-451/">canon 28</a>). By Justinian's day
        the ideal was a <strong>pentarchy</strong> — Rome, Constantinople, Alexandria,
        Antioch, Jerusalem — five patriarchs over one church, disputes settled in
        council, on the pattern of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:1-29">Acts 15</a>. It is worth noticing who
        convened the great councils: not popes but emperors, from Nicaea onward.</p>

        <p>Rome's own trajectory — from honored church "presiding in love" to claimed
        universal jurisdiction and, in 1870, defined infallibility — is a study of its
        own on this site: <a href="../papacy/">The Papacy: What the Evidence
        Supports</a>. One marker belongs here because it dates the fork in the road:
        when the patriarch of Constantinople took the title "ecumenical (universal)
        bishop" around 588, Gregory the Great — bishop of Rome, and a genuinely holy
        man — denounced the very idea of a universal bishop as a "name of blasphemy,"
        writing that whoever exalts himself as universal priest is a forerunner of
        Antichrist (Letters V.20; VII.33). Within two centuries his successors were
        using equivalent titles without embarrassment.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Reformation reset — and the three Protestant polities</h3>

        <p>The Reformers attacked the medieval hierarchy at its root — the priesthood.
        If Christ's sacrifice is finished
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:14">Hebrews 10:14</a>) and every believer
        has direct access through the one Mediator
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:5">1 Timothy 2:5</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 4:16">Hebrews 4:16</a>), then ministry is a
        calling to serve the word and sacraments
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:11-12">Ephesians 4:11–12</a>), not a
        sacrificing caste. But Protestants divided over what structure Scripture
        actually warrants, and the three answers still organize the Protestant
        world:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Episcopal</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Bishops retained — reformed, not abolished.</div>
          <p>Anglicans (and Scandinavian Lutherans, and later Methodists with their
          superintendent-bishops) kept the historic three-fold order as ancient and
          edifying, while denying it makes grace dependent on a succession. Warrant
          claimed: the apostles' own oversight role, Timothy and Titus as proto-bishops
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 1:3">1 Timothy 1:3</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5">Titus 1:5</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Presbyterian</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Rule by councils of elders.</div>
          <p>The Reformed churches read the New Testament's plural eldership
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 14:23">Acts 14:23</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:17">Acts 20:17</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 4:14">1 Timothy 4:14</a> — "the council of
          elders") and the Jerusalem council
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:1-29">Acts 15</a>) as a system: teaching
          and ruling elders (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 5:17">1 Timothy
          5:17</a>) in graded assemblies — session, presbytery, general assembly
          (<a href="../../library/read/westminster-confession/">Westminster
          XXXI</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Congregational</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Each congregation complete under Christ.</div>
          <p>Congregationalists and Baptists concluded that every local church, gathered
          around word and table with its own elders and deacons, possesses full church
          power under Christ — discipline is the congregation's act
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:17">Matthew 18:17</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 5:4-5">1 Corinthians 5:4–5</a>), and
          Christ is present where two or three gather in his name
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:20">Matthew 18:20</a>;
          <a href="../../library/read/savoy-declaration/">Savoy Declaration, 1658</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The whole arc in one sentence</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">From plural local elders (1st century), to one bishop per city against the heretics (2nd), to priest and altar (3rd), to imperial diocese and patriarchate (4th–6th), to the medieval two-headed contest of pope and council, to the Reformation's return toward the earlier patterns — every step had its reason, and every step is documented. The question each branch answers differently is which steps were faithful development of <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:11-12">Ephesians 4:11–12</a>, and which quietly traded the towel of <a class="ref" data-ref="John 13:14-15">John 13:14–15</a> for the throne of <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 20:25">Matthew 20:25</a>.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /order-growth -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 10: WHEN THINGS SPLIT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="splits">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">When Things Split</h2>

        <p>Every division in this section should be read against one text:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          I am not asking on behalf of them alone, but also on behalf of those who will
          believe in Me through their message, that all of them may be one, as You,
          Father, are in Me, and I am in You. May they also be in Us, so that the world
          may believe that You sent Me.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="John 17:20-21">John 17:20–21</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>And one sober counterweight: the apostles themselves expected division to
        expose what is genuine — "there must in fact be divisions among you, so that
        those of approved character may be evident among you" (<a class="ref"
        data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:19">1 Corinthians 11:19</a>) — and commanded
        separation from another gospel
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 1:8-9">Galatians 1:8–9</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 16:17">Romans 16:17</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="2 John 10-11">2 John 10–11</a>). Church history's
        splits are therefore not all the same kind of event: some guarded the gospel,
        some tore the seamless robe over power and pride, and most braided doctrine
        together with language, empire, and personality so tightly that only God can
        fully separate the strands. The four great fractures:</p>

        <div class="tg-timeline">
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">431</span><span><strong>The Church of the East</strong> parts after Ephesus — the churches of the Persian empire, culturally Syriac and politically outside Rome's world, do not accept the council that condemned Nestorius. Their missionaries reach India and Tang-dynasty China by 635</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">451</span><span><strong>The Oriental Orthodox</strong> (Coptic, Syriac, Armenian, Ethiopian, and others) decline Chalcedon's "two natures," holding the miaphysite confession of "one incarnate nature of the Word" — for them, Chalcedon divided Christ</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1054</span><span><strong>East and West</strong> excommunicate each other's legates — the visible crack of a long drift: the filioque added to the creed in the West, unleavened bread, clerical celibacy, and above all the papal claim to universal jurisdiction versus the East's conciliar pentarchy</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1204</span><span>The Fourth Crusade sacks Constantinople — after which the East–West schism is no longer a clerical quarrel but a wound in the peoples. The mutual anathemas of 1054 were lifted in 1965; the division remains</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1517</span><span><strong>The Reformation</strong> — indulgences light the fuse, but the charges are authority (Scripture over pope and council) and justification (faith alone). Rome answers at Trent (1545–63); Western Christendom is permanently plural</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1520s–1600s</span><span>Protestantism divides internally: Lutheran vs. Reformed at Marburg (1529); Anabaptists reject the state-church entirely (1525); the Church of England takes its via media; Arminians and Calvinists divide at Dort (1619)</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1600s–1900s</span><span>Renewal movements become denominations: Baptists (1609), Quakers, Pietists, Methodists (Wesley's societies separate after 1784), Restorationists, the Salvation Army — most beginning as reform movements <em>inside</em> an existing church</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1906</span><span><strong>Pentecostalism</strong> erupts from the Azusa Street revival — within a century the fastest-growing Christian family on earth, reshaping the faith's center of gravity toward the global South</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A note of honest hope</span>
          <p>Not every ancient split is as deep as it looked. Modern dialogue between
          Chalcedonian and non-Chalcedonian churches (the 1994 Common Christological
          Declaration with the Church of the East; agreed statements with the Oriental
          Orthodox) found that much of the 5th-century rupture was terminology and
          politics wearing the clothes of doctrine — both sides confess one Christ,
          truly God and truly man. The 1999 Joint Declaration on justification
          (<a href="#sin-grace">§ 4</a>) is a smaller step of the same kind. Real
          differences remain — this study has not minimized them — but the record
          cautions against assuming that every wall stands on bedrock.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /splits -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 11: THE BRANCHES TODAY
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="branches">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Branches Today</h2>

        <p>Set side by side, the living branches differ most sharply on exactly the
        three axes this study has traced — where final authority lies, how a sinner is
        made right with God, and what the sacraments and the ministry are:</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Roman Catholic</th><th>Eastern Orthodox</th><th>Magisterial Protestant<br>(Lutheran · Reformed · Anglican)</th><th>Free-church Protestant<br>(Baptist · Pentecostal · independent)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Final authority</strong></td><td>Scripture and Tradition, authentically interpreted by the magisterium under the pope</td><td>Scripture within Holy Tradition — the councils, liturgy, and consensus of the fathers</td><td>Scripture alone as final norm; creeds and confessions as subordinate standards</td><td>Scripture alone; confessions lightly held, congregational conscience prominent</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Justification</strong></td><td>Grace infused, making the believer really just; increased by cooperation and sacraments; lost by mortal sin</td><td>Rarely framed as a courtroom question — salvation as union with God (<em>theosis</em>), a lifelong synergy of grace and will</td><td>Declared righteous by grace alone through faith alone on account of Christ alone; good works the fruit, not the ground</td><td>Same confession as the magisterial churches, with strong emphasis on personal conversion and new birth</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Sacraments</strong></td><td>Seven; grace conferred <em>ex opere operato</em></td><td>The holy mysteries — commonly counted seven, boundary left soft</td><td>Two (baptism and the supper), effectual signs received by faith</td><td>Two ordinances, acts of obedience and testimony</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Baptism</strong></td><td>Infants and converts; regenerates</td><td>Infants and converts; regenerates (baptism, chrismation, communion together)</td><td>Infants of believers and converts; Lutherans confess regeneration, Reformed a covenant sign and seal</td><td>Professing believers only, normally by immersion</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>The Supper</strong></td><td>Transubstantiation; the mass a re-presented sacrifice</td><td>True body and blood as undefined mystery</td><td>Sacramental union (Lutheran) or true spiritual feeding (Reformed/Anglican)</td><td>Memorial and communion of the body</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Order</strong></td><td>Pope, bishops in apostolic succession, priests, deacons</td><td>Autocephalous churches of bishops in succession, governed in synod — no universal head but Christ</td><td>Episcopal (Anglican, some Lutheran), presbyterian (Reformed), with ministry as word-and-sacrament office</td><td>Congregational — local elders/pastors and deacons under Christ</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Around that table sit families the columns compress. The <strong>Oriental
        Orthodox</strong> (~60 million — Copts, Ethiopians, Armenians, Syriacs) and the
        small, heroic <strong>Assyrian Church of the East</strong> stand nearest the
        Eastern Orthodox column with their own ancient rites and christological
        vocabulary. <strong>Methodists</strong> bridge the magisterial and free-church
        columns — episcopal in order, Arminian in grace, warm-hearted by Wesleyan
        conviction. <strong>Pentecostals and charismatics</strong>, now the largest
        Protestant stream worldwide, add to the free-church pattern a conviction that
        the Spirit's gifts of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:8-10">1 Corinthians 12:8–10</a> and
        the empowering of <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:8">Acts 1:8</a> are for
        today. In rough contemporary numbers: some 1.3–1.4 billion Catholics, well over
        a billion Protestants of all families, 200–260 million Eastern Orthodox — a
        reminder that the church's center of gravity now lies in Africa, Asia, and
        Latin America, among believers who inherited none of these quarrels and must
        make the ancient faith their own
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:39">Acts 2:39</a>).</p>

      </section><!-- /branches -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 12: THE THROUGH LINE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="through-line">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Through Line of the Faithful</h2>

        <p>After eleven sections of dates, definitions, and divisions, step back and
        look at what has <em>not</em> changed. Strip any era's church — 2nd-century
        Smyrna, 9th-century Constantinople, 16th-century Geneva, a house church in
        modern China — down to its actual life, and the same four marks appear, the
        ones fixed in the church's first summer:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          They devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and to the fellowship, to
          the breaking of bread and to prayer.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:42">Acts 2:42</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The apostles' teaching — the same Scriptures read in every branch, the same
        27-book New Testament in all of them. The fellowship — a people belonging to
        one another across every line the world draws
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 3:28">Galatians 3:28</a>). The breaking of
        bread — the table kept in every tradition, however differently understood,
        proclaiming the same death "until He comes"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:26">1 Corinthians 11:26</a>). And
        prayer — the Lord's Prayer on the lips of every family of Christians every day
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 6:9-13">Matthew 6:9–13</a>), the Psalms as
        the shared songbook of them all. Add the one baptism in the triune name, and
        the Nicene faith — Trinity, incarnation, cross for our sins, bodily
        resurrection, coming judgment, the Spirit, the church, the life of the world to
        come — and you are looking at what every branch in the table above confesses
        <em>together</em>, the deposit Paul delivered "as of first importance"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 15:3-4">1 Corinthians 15:3–4</a>).</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          There is one body and one Spirit, just as you were called to one hope when
          you were called; one Lord, one faith, one baptism; one God and Father of all,
          who is over all and through all and in all.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:4-6">Ephesians 4:4–6</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>But the through line of this study is not, in the end, a list of doctrines.
        It is a people. The companion study
        <a href="../walk-through-church-history/">A Walk Through Church History</a>
        traces it era by era: beneath the visible institutions — sometimes carried by
        them, sometimes surviving in spite of them — runs the invisible church, "God's
        firm foundation… bearing this seal: 'The Lord knows those who are His'"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:19">2 Timothy 2:19</a>). Its members are
        found in every one of the branches, and in every one of the centuries: the
        believers who received the word, loved the brothers, kept the table, endured to
        the end (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 24:13">Matthew 24:13</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 3:14">1 John 3:14</a>). Scripture names them a
        "great cloud of witnesses" surrounding the living
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 12:1">Hebrews 12:1</a>), and shows where the
        whole story arrives — not at one denomination vindicated, but at one multitude
        gathered:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          After this I looked and saw a multitude too large to count, from every nation
          and tribe and people and tongue, standing before the throne and before the
          Lamb… And they cried out in a loud voice: "Salvation to our God, who sits on
          the throne, and to the Lamb!"
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 7:9-10">Revelation 7:9–10</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">How, then, to hold it all</h3>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Hold convictions seriously</span>
          <p>This study has shown that the differences are real — grace, sacrament, and
          authority are not trivia, and pretending all views are the same honors none
          of them. Search the Scriptures and be "fully convinced in his own mind"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14:5">Romans 14:5</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:11">Acts 17:11</a>); contend earnestly for
          the faith once delivered (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 3">Jude 3</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Hold the family wide</span>
          <p>Pursue "righteousness, faith, love, and peace, together with those who
          call on the Lord out of a pure heart"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:22">2 Timothy 2:22</a>) — Paul's
          description of the fellowship cuts across every later boundary. The
          17th-century peacemaker's maxim (Rupertus Meldenius, c. 1627) remains the
          best one-line rule ever written for a divided Christendom: <em>in essentials,
          unity; in non-essentials, liberty; in all things, charity</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 13:2">1 Corinthians 13:2</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Hold history without fear</span>
          <p>Nothing in this study needed to be hidden for the faith to survive the
          telling. The structures developed; the deposit endured; the gates of Hades
          did not prevail (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:18">Matthew 16:18</a>).
          The same Lord who kept his word through councils and schisms, empires and
          reformations, is keeping it still — "and surely I am with you always, even to
          the end of the age"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 28:20">Matthew 28:20</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Now to Him who is able to keep you from stumbling and to present you unblemished
          in His glorious presence, with great joy — to the only God our Savior be glory,
          majesty, dominion, and authority through Jesus Christ our Lord before all time,
          and now, and for all eternity. Amen.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 24-25">Jude 24–25</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

      </section><!-- /through-line -->

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Documents of the Christian Church</cite>, ed. Henry Bettenson &amp; Chris
          Maunder. Oxford. <em>The one-volume primary-source anthology — creeds, canons,
          bulls, and confessions in order, so every claim in this study can be checked
          against the documents.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Early Christian Doctrines</cite>, J. N. D. Kelly (1958).
          <em>The standard scholarly survey of how each doctrine developed to
          Chalcedon — careful, fair, and relentlessly source-based.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Creeds of Christendom</cite>, Philip Schaff (1877, 3 vols).
          <em>Every major creed and confession with history and Greek/Latin texts.
          Public domain.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Christian Tradition</cite>, Jaroslav Pelikan (1971–89, 5 vols).
          <em>The magisterial history of doctrine — "what the church of Jesus Christ
          believes, teaches, and confesses on the basis of the word of God."</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>An Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine</cite>, John Henry
          Newman (1845). <em>The strongest case that doctrine legitimately grows —
          read it beside a Protestant reply (e.g. Cunningham) and judge the tests for
          yourself. Public domain.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

